  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA few showers will exit to the south through this evening. A \ncold front extends from near Pittsburgh to north of Columbus at \n3 PM and will continue to move south tonight as high pressure \nbuilds across the Upper Great Lakes. Scattered thunderstorms \nhave filled in towards Columbus and should mostly remain south \nof the local area. \n\nThursday will be dry to start the day. Low pressure is expected to \ntrack out of the Mid-Mississippi Valley towards lower Michigan by \nThursday evening. Initially there will be a dry low level flow out \nof the east with isentropic ascent increasing aloft during the \nafternoon. Showers will spread west to late afternoon through the \nevening as the warm front lifts north to near Lake Erie. A few \nthunderstorms will also be possible during the evening, especially \nacross the north near the front.  \n\nThe Storm Prediction Center does have a Marginal Risk of Severe \nThunderstorms highlighted across portions of northern Ohio. The \ntiming for this risk is late Thursday night with any thunderstorms \noccurring along or just ahead of the cold front generally after 2 AM \nFriday. The wind field will be strong initially with 40-45 knots of \nsheer aloft. The limiting factor for thunderstorms will be \ninstability with surface based CAPE of only a few hundred joules. \nHave adjusted the forecast to indicate a non-diurnal temperature \ntrend with temperatures peaking on Friday during the early morning \nhours ahead of the cold front. Even without thunderstorms, some \nstronger wind gusts may accompany showers and there is potential for \na few wind gusts of 40-50 mph. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLow pressure will continue across the eastern Great \nLakes on Friday, pulling a strong cold front east across the local \narea. Showers will accompany the front crossing the area during the \nmorning. Strong cold air advection will occur behind the front with \n850mb temperatures falling by about 10C between 06-18Z Friday. The \nsurface low however is not as deep as previous runs and the upper \nlevel trough remains positively tilted and elongated to the deep \nsouth as it crosses the Central Great Lakes. Mixing depths behind \nthe front on Friday are only up to about 4K feet with winds of 30-35 \nknots through that depth. Wind gusts of 30-35 mph are expected \nthrough the day on Friday with temperatures falling into the 50s by \nafternoon. \n\nA secondary trough swings south across Lake Erie during the evening \nwith winds shifting from northwest to northerly. This is the window \nof strongest winds on Lake Erie and lakeshore areas will see winds \ngusting to 35-45 mph Friday night. In addition, this secondary \ntrough will result in a flare up of lake enhanced rain showers. \nCoverage of showers will be fairly high for several hours then \nbecoming focused across north central Ohio overnight as winds shift \naround to the north/northeast. This includes flow off Lake Huron \nwhich prolong scattered showers through Friday night and extended a \nlow chance pop into Saturday morning for favored areas. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe coldest air of the season arrives behind the cold front. \nTemperatures at 850mb will range from -2 to -6C at 12Z Saturday \nbefore gradually starting to rebound over the weekend as the \ntrough shifts east. Temperatures on Friday night will tend to \nbottom out in the upper 30s for inland portions of northern Ohio\nbut may reach the lower 30s in Northwest Pennsylvania. In \ngeneral expecting precipitation to fall as nearly all rain as we\nlose ice in the cloud aloft late tonight. \n\nHigh pressure builds overhead Saturday into Saturday night which \nwill result in a favorable radiational cooling set-up. Saturday \nnight lows are forecast to be near freezing for much of inland \nnorthern Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania. This may result in frost \nor the first freeze of the season and potentially end the growing \nseason for some locations.",
